.TH RIG 1 "2026-09-20" "Knowledge Islands" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
\&
.PP
rig \- describe and manage a personal working setup
.SH SYNOPSIS
\&
.br
.B rig show
.RI [ \-\-profile " NAME" ]
.br
.B rig list
.RI [ \-\-category " ID" ]
.RI [ \-\-profile " NAME" ]
.br
.B rig explain
.I TOOL|service:ID|scheduled-job:ID|setting:ID|dock:ID
.br
.B rig status
.RI [ \-\-profile " NAME" ]
.RI [ \-\-unmanaged ]
.br
.B rig doctor
.RI [ \-\-profile " NAME" ]
.br
.B rig apply
.RI [ \-\-profile " NAME" ]
.RI [ \-\-scope " tools|resources|all" ]
.RI [ \-\-dry-run ]
.br
.B rig bootstrap
.RI [ \-\-profile " NAME" ]
.RI [ \-\-scope " tools|resources|all" ]
.RI [ \-\-dry-run ]
.br
.BI "rig run " PROVIDER " " ACTION
.RI [ \-\- " ARGUMENT..." ]
.br
.BI "rig export " PUBLICATION
.BI " \-\-output " DIRECTORY
.br
.BI "rig publish " PUBLICATION
.br
.B rig clean
.RI [ \-\-dry-run ]
.br
.B rig diag
.br
.BI "rig completion " bash|zsh
.br
.B rig help
.RB [ \-h | \-\-help ]
.br
.B rig
.RB [ \-h | \-\-help | \-V | \-\-version ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
\&
.PP
Rig describes the tools and managed resources that make up a person's working
setup, explains why they belong, and compares the selected setup with the
current machine. Managed resources include services, scheduled jobs, typed
macOS settings, and semantic Dock layouts.
.PP
The catalogue records tool meaning. Managed resources record declarative
machine intent. Profiles select tools and resources for machines, roles, or
contexts. Rig chooses its built-in integration from each declaration's
provider identity; explicitly declared external providers extend that built-in
set. State compares resolved intent with native observations.
.PP
Catalogue queries and diagnostics parse inert configuration and do not invoke
provider code. Status and doctor invoke observation capabilities.
Apply and bootstrap cross the explicit mutation boundary only after complete
plan preflight.
Run crosses an explicit provider-action trust boundary after resolving either
a built-in operation or a declared external-provider action. Publish
crosses a network-capable trust boundary only after export validation.
Package and configuration systems retain authority over their native
manifests, resolution, configuration, and state.
.SH USER WORKFLOW
\&
.PP
Declare a catalogue and one or more profiles. Use
.BR "rig show" ,
.BR "rig list" ,
and
.B rig explain
to understand the declaration without invoking providers.
.PP
Use
.B rig diag
to inspect Rig itself,
.B rig doctor
for a concise health assessment, and
.B rig status
for the complete expected-versus-observed comparison.
.PP
Review
.B rig apply \-\-dry-run
before allowing native-system changes. Use
.B rig apply
or
.B rig bootstrap
only when the complete plan is expected, or select
.BR "\-\-scope tools" " or " "\-\-scope resources"
for one part of reconciliation. Public sharing follows the separate
offline
.B rig export
and explicit
.B rig publish
boundary.
.SH INSTALLATION
\&
.PP
Install the
.B v0.2.0
public preview:
.PP
.nf
curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/knowledgeislands/tools-rig/v0.2.0/install.sh | bash
.fi
.PP
Pin an exact release explicitly:
.PP
.nf
curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/knowledgeislands/tools-rig/v0.2.0/install.sh | bash -s -- v0.2.0
.fi
.PP
The installer writes the executable to
.I ~/.local/bin
and the manual beneath
.I ${XDG_DATA_HOME:-$HOME/.local/share}/man/man1
by default. Set
.B RIG_INSTALL_DIR
or
.B RIG_MAN_INSTALL_DIR
to choose different locations. Set
.B RIG_VERSION
to an exact
.B vX.Y.Z
release for automation compatibility. A positional version takes precedence.
Without either form, the installer discovers the latest GitHub release.
.SH COMMANDS
\&
.TP
.B rig show
Describe the resolved default profile with aligned tool and concise managed
resource tables.
.TP
.BI "rig show \-\-profile " NAME
Describe the named resolved profile with aligned tool and operational-resource
tables without changing the configured default.
.TP
.B rig list
List every declared catalogue tool in deterministic order.
.TP
.BI "rig list \-\-category " ID
Narrow the list to tools in the declared category.
.TP
.BI "rig list \-\-profile " NAME
Narrow the list to tools selected by the resolved profile.
The category and profile filters may be combined.
.TP
.BI "rig explain " TOOL|service:ID|scheduled-job:ID|setting:ID|dock:ID
Show a tool's identity and installation metadata, or a qualified operational
resource's purpose, rationale, native ownership, desired values, policies,
tool dependencies, and profile membership. Dock explanations include ordered
item details.
.TP
.B rig status
Compare every tool and operational resource in the resolved default profile
with its selected native observation. Catalogue-only tools are reported as
neutral unavailable rows; stale resource receipts are pending retirement.
.TP
.BI "rig status \-\-profile " NAME
Compare a named resolved profile without changing the configured default.
.TP
.B rig status \-\-unmanaged
Additionally ask each selected inventory source to enumerate its native
domain, and report each observed identity that no tool installation declares.
Built-in inventory needs no configuration declaration; external inventory
must be explicitly allowed. Unmanaged rows are informational and do not make
the result unhealthy. Reverse reconciliation answers the opposite question to
the tool table: not whether declared software is installed, but whether
installed software was ever declared.
.TP
.B rig doctor
Check the resolved default profile and print one compact operational health
answer. Doctor checks effective XDG path accessibility, native-manager and
external-provider availability, and selected tool and resource observations.
Catalogue-only and
incompatible-platform tools are informational.
.TP
.BI "rig doctor \-\-profile " NAME
Check a named resolved profile without changing the configured default.
Doctor invokes only fixed built-in observation operations or external
observation operations explicitly allowed for selected providers. It never
applies, repairs, publishes, or invokes unselected providers.
.TP
.B rig apply
Preflight every selected bound tool, operational resource, stale receipt
entry, and receipt target before invoking providers. Tools run in
dependency-first order, followed by selected resources and stale retirement.
A failure skips only dependent work; independent work continues. The atomic
resource receipt advances only after the complete resource plan succeeds.
.TP
.BI "rig apply \-\-profile " NAME
Apply a named resolved profile without changing the configured default.
.TP
.B rig apply \-\-dry-run
Perform complete preflight and print planned work without invoking a provider.
.TP
.BI "rig apply \-\-scope " tools|resources|all
Limit reconciliation to selected tool installations, to managed resources and
their stale receipts, or to both. The default is
.BR all .
The scope changes execution, not profile resolution or declaration ownership.
.TP
.B rig bootstrap
Run Rig's native bootstrap lifecycle for the configured bootstrap profile.
Rig identifies, verifies, and preflights required native managers before using
the same dependency order, complete preflight, dispatch, reporting, and
failure boundaries as
.BR "rig apply" .
Bootstrap is not a provider and requires no synthetic setup tools.
An explicit
.BI "\-\-profile " NAME
takes precedence over
.BR bootstrap-profile ;
when that field is absent, bootstrap uses
.BR default-profile .
.B \-\-dry-run
prints the shared plan without invoking a provider.
The
.B \-\-scope
option has the same values and meaning as for
.BR "rig apply" .
.TP
.BI "rig run " PROVIDER " " ACTION " [\-\- " ARGUMENT... "]"
Invoke one built-in provider operation or one explicitly declared external
provider action. External actions use their declared observation or mutation
operation. Configured arguments precede accepted caller arguments. Rig passes
provider output through and preserves its native exit status.
.TP
.BI "rig export " PUBLICATION " \-\-output " DIRECTORY
Generate a deterministic, versioned
.I rig.json
artifact from the publication's explicitly selected, platform-neutral profile.
Export invokes no provider, publisher, or network command.
.TP
.BI "rig publish " PUBLICATION
Render and validate one isolated public-data export beneath the effective Rig
cache, then invoke the publication's selected trusted external publisher exactly
once.
The publisher and receiving website own presentation, credentials, destination,
deployment, and rollback.
.TP
.B rig clean
.RI [ \-\-dry-run ]
Remove complete retained publication exports and resumable cleanup claims that
Rig can prove it owns. With
.BR \-\-dry-run ,
perform the same discovery and validation but report
.B would-remove
without changing the filesystem. This command does not load configuration,
invoke providers, or inspect provider-native caches.
.TP
.B rig diag
Print a non-mutating diagnostic snapshot containing Rig and Bash versions,
the invoked executable, active platform, effective XDG directories, root
configuration and fragment count, configuration validity, schema, and default
profile when available. No provider is invoked.
.TP
.BI "rig completion " bash|zsh
Print completion source for the selected shell. With
.B \-h
or
.BR \-\-help ,
print command usage without generating completion source.
.TP
.B rig help
Show the compact top-level command summary. Use
.BI "rig " COMMAND " \-\-help"
for detailed command syntax. The optional
.B \-h
and
.B \-\-help
forms produce the same output.
.SH SHELL COMPLETION
\&
.PP
The
.B rig completion bash
and
.B rig completion zsh
print completion definitions for the shipped command and option surface.
.PP
For Bash, source the definition from the shell configuration:
.PP
.nf
source <(rig completion bash)
.fi
.PP
For Zsh, write the definition to a file named
.I _rig
in a directory on
.B fpath
before
.B compinit
runs. For example:
.PP
.nf
mkdir -p ~/.zsh/completions
rig completion zsh > ~/.zsh/completions/_rig
fpath=(~/.zsh/completions $fpath)
autoload -Uz compinit && compinit
.fi
.SH OPTIONS
\&
.TP
.BR \-h , " \-\-help"
Show command help.
.TP
.BR \-V , " \-\-version"
Print the Rig version.
.SH CONFIGURATION
\&
.PP
Rig reads an optional
.I ${RIG_CONFIG_HOME}/rig.toml
first when present and then regular
.I ${RIG_CONFIG_HOME}/conf.d/*.toml
fragments in bytewise filename order. At least one source must exist, and the
merged declaration must contain exactly one
.B [rig]
table.
When
.B RIG_CONFIG_HOME
is unset, its default is
.IR ${XDG_CONFIG_HOME:-$HOME/.config}/rig .
.PP
Configuration uses schema 1's bounded TOML grammar: named tables, the integer
schema value, single-line basic strings, single-line string arrays, blank lines,
and comments. Accepted files are valid TOML. Rig rejects unused TOML features
and does not source configuration, evaluate commands, interpret shell syntax,
or perform general environment expansion.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
\&
.TP
.B RIG_CONFIG_HOME
Override the Rig configuration directory.
.TP
.B RIG_DATA_HOME
Override the Rig data directory.
.TP
.B RIG_STATE_HOME
Override the Rig state directory.
.TP
.B RIG_CACHE_HOME
Override the Rig cache directory.
.TP
.B RIG_PLATFORM
Override the active catalogue platform used by profile and installation
resolution.
By default Rig maps Bash
.B OSTYPE
values beginning with
.B darwin
to
.B macos
and values beginning with
.B linux
to
.BR linux .
.TP
.B RIG_PROGRESS
Set to
.B always
to retain line-oriented configuration and provider progress when stderr is
redirected, or
.B never
to suppress it. The default is
.BR auto ,
which reports progress only when stderr is a terminal. Progress never changes
the deterministic report on stdout.
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Set the base configuration directory when
.B RIG_CONFIG_HOME
is unset. The fallback is
.IR $HOME/.config .
.TP
.B XDG_DATA_HOME
Set the base data directory when
.B RIG_DATA_HOME
is unset. The fallback is
.IR $HOME/.local/share .
.TP
.B XDG_STATE_HOME
Set the base state directory when
.B RIG_STATE_HOME
is unset. The fallback is
.IR $HOME/.local/state .
.TP
.B XDG_CACHE_HOME
Set the base cache directory when
.B RIG_CACHE_HOME
is unset. The fallback is
.IR $HOME/.cache .
.SH FILES
\&
.TP
.I ${XDG_CONFIG_HOME:-$HOME/.config}/rig/rig.toml
Optional root configuration file. Schema 1 uses
.B [rig]
with
.B schema
and
.B default-profile
fields, plus optional
.BR bootstrap-profile ;
.B [category.ID]
with
.B name
and
.BR purpose ;
.B [tool.ID]
with
.BR name ,
.BR category ,
.BR purpose ,
.BR rationale ,
and a non-empty
.B platforms
string array, plus optional
.BR requires ,
.BR related ,
.BR alternatives ,
and
.B artifacts
string arrays; and
.B [profile.ID]
with
.B profiles
and
.B tools
string arrays, plus optional
.BR services ,
.BR scheduled-jobs ,
.BR settings ,
and
.B docks
arrays. Tool
.B install.*
fields retain their native locator and argument data without executing it while
configuration is loaded. Source-authored
.B [binding.*]
tables are invalid.
.B [service.ID]
and
.B [scheduled-job.ID]
declare operational resources. Both require
.BR name ,
.BR purpose ,
.BR rationale ,
.BR provider ,
.BR locator ,
.BR desired-state ,
non-empty
.B platforms
and
.B program
arrays, and accept
.BR requires ,
.BR environment ,
.BR working-directory ,
.BR standard-output ,
and
.BR standard-error .
Services accept
.B restart-policy
and
.BR start-policy .
Scheduled jobs require exactly one of
.B schedule.calendar
or
.B schedule.interval
and accept
.B run-policy
and
.BR priority .
Profiles select resources with
.B services
and
.B scheduled-jobs
arrays. Resource requirements name tools.
.PP
.B [setting.ID]
declares one typed macOS defaults value. It requires
.BR name ,
.BR purpose ,
.BR rationale ,
.BR provider ,
.BR domain ,
.BR key ,
.BR value-type ,
.BR value ,
and a non-empty
.B platforms
array, and accepts
.BR requires .
The provider is the built-in
.BR macos-defaults ;
.B value-type
is
.BR bool ,
.BR int ,
.BR float ,
or
.BR string .
.PP
.B [dock.ID]
declares one semantic macOS Dock order. It requires
.BR name ,
.BR purpose ,
.BR rationale ,
.BR provider ,
and non-empty
.B platforms
and
.B items
arrays, and accepts
.BR requires .
The provider is the built-in
.BR macos-dock .
Each ordered item names one
.B [dock-item.ID]
table with
.B kind
and
.BR path .
Kind
.B application
names an application path. Kind
.B folder
may also declare its
.B view
and
.B display
semantics. Profiles select settings and Dock layouts through their respective
arrays; Dock items are selected only by a Dock layout.
.PP
Provider identities built into Rig need no
.B [provider.ID]
table and their supported operations cannot be granted or removed by
configuration. The built-in identities are
.BR homebrew ,
.BR uv ,
.BR chezmoi ,
.BR direct-download ,
.BR launchd ,
.BR macos-defaults ,
and
.BR macos-dock .
Where documented, a built-in provider table may override only its native
.BR executable ,
.BR manifest ,
or literal
.B arguments
configuration; it cannot declare an adapter or capabilities.
.PP
An identity not built into Rig is an external provider. Its
.B [provider.ID]
table requires a non-empty
.B capabilities
operation allow-list, and accepts an
.B executable
and literal
.BR arguments .
If
.B executable
is omitted, Rig resolves exactly
.IR ${RIG_DATA_HOME}/providers/ID .
Rig does not search for alternatives or execute adjacent files.
.B [tool.ID]
requires
.B install.provider
when materialised, together with
.B install.kind
and
.B install.locator
and accepts optional scalar
.B install.destination
and
.B install.checksum
fields plus
.B install.platforms
and
.B install.arguments
string arrays. A tool with no install provider is catalogue-only. Destination
and checksum are restricted to direct-download installations.
.B [publication.ID]
requires
.BR profile ,
.BR title ,
.BR base-url ,
and
.BR publisher .
Offline export does not invoke the publisher. Explicit publication requires
an explicitly declared external provider with the exact
.B publish
capability.
.B [action.PROVIDER.NAME]
requires
.BR mode ,
and
.BR description .
It accepts
.BR platforms ,
.BR arguments ,
and
.BR allowed-arguments ,
and optional
.B argument-policy
and
.B resource-kinds
fields. Resource-aware actions require provider argument policy and accept one
selected qualified resource before any caller arguments. The mode is exactly
.B observe
or
.BR mutate .
Only the executable
external-provider boundary dispatches declared actions. Built-in operations do
not need action declarations. Argument policy defaults to Rig's exact
allowlist; value
.B provider
delegates validation to that external provider.
.PP
The
.B base-url
must be an absolute HTTP or HTTPS URL without a query or fragment. Rig appends
a trailing slash when omitted and projects it as
.B publication.canonical_url
metadata. Rig does not generate presentation or navigation.
.PP
A minimal catalogue is:
.PP
.nf
[rig]
schema = 1
default-profile = "default"

[category.navigation]
name = "Navigation"
purpose = "Move through Knowledge Islands"

[tool.mgit]
name = "MGit"
category = "navigation"
purpose = "Navigate related repositories"
rationale = "Keeps repository context visible"
platforms = ["macos"]
install.provider = "homebrew"
install.kind = "formula"
install.locator = "mgit"
install.platforms = ["macos"]

[profile.default]
tools = ["mgit"]
.fi
.TP
.I ${XDG_CONFIG_HOME:-$HOME/.config}/rig/conf.d/*.toml
Optional configuration fragments.
.TP
.I ${XDG_DATA_HOME:-$HOME/.local/share}/rig
Default data directory.
.TP
.I ${XDG_DATA_HOME:-$HOME/.local/share}/rig/providers/ID
Conventional executable for an explicitly declared external provider whose
table omits
.BR executable .
.TP
.I ${XDG_STATE_HOME:-$HOME/.local/state}/rig
Default state directory.
.TP
.I ${XDG_STATE_HOME:-$HOME/.local/state}/rig/resources/PLATFORM.tsv
Atomic reconciliation receipt for resources last successfully managed on one
platform. Each tab-separated row stores only provider, resource kind, identity,
and locator; it is not observed state or declaration authority.
.TP
.I ${XDG_CACHE_HOME:-$HOME/.cache}/rig
Default cache directory.
.SH PUBLIC DATA EXPORT
\&
.PP
The
.B rig export
writes exactly one regular, non-symlink
.I rig.json
file. Its top-level
.B format
is
.B rig-publication
and its integer
.B version
is 1. It contains publication metadata, profile identity, selected categories,
and selected tool identity, name, purpose, rationale, declared platforms, and
closed relationships. Category, tool, platform, and relationship values use
deterministic bytewise ordering.
.PP
The public profile is resolved independently of the active host platform while
each tool retains its declared platforms. Relationships are included only when
both endpoints are selected. Provider configuration and tool installation
metadata, private profiles, observed state, credentials, and machine paths are
not rendered.
Consumers should check both
.B format
and
.B version
before interpreting the remaining document.
.PP
Rig first renders a complete sibling staging tree and then replaces the named
output directory. It never merges with an existing tree. For safety, Rig
rejects output targets that are
.BR / ,
.BR . ,
.BR .. ,
symlinks, or non-directories. Review the generated tree before passing it to a
separate hosting or publisher system.
.SH PUBLICATION
\&
.B rig publish
performs the same validated public projection in an isolated directory beneath
.IR ${RIG_CACHE_HOME:-${XDG_CACHE_HOME:-$HOME/.cache}/rig}/publish/staging ,
then crosses one explicit network-capable trust boundary. It invokes no fallback
publisher and passes the selected provider one absolute complete-tree path.
.PP
Successful publication removes the staged tree. A publisher failure preserves
its native exit status and retains the complete tree, reporting its path on
standard error. An interruption before the export is complete removes its
incomplete known files, invokes no publisher, and returns the conventional
signal status without reporting a retained export. Once complete, an
interrupted publication instead retains and reports the tree. Validation,
staging, and render failures invoke no publisher.
.PP
Cleanup revalidates the canonical cache parent and operates relative to that
pinned directory. It unlinks only
.I rig.json
before removing the empty directory. A substituted parent, symlink,
unexpected file, or other unsafe shape fails closed without recursive traversal.
.SH CACHE MAINTENANCE
\&
.B rig clean
maintains only cache data owned by Rig. Active publication work remains beneath
.IR publish/staging .
Complete exports preserved after publisher failure or interruption remain
beneath
.IR publish/retained
until explicit cleanup. Cleanup claims an eligible tree beneath
.IR publish/cleanup ,
revalidates its exact one-file shape, unlinks only
.IR rig.json ,
and removes only the now-empty directory.
.PP
Retained artifacts have no automatic age or count expiry. A dry run prints the
same tab-separated
.BR CLASS ,
.BR STATE ,
.BR ACTION ,
and
.B PATH
report without creating, moving, or removing paths. Legacy flat entries,
symlinks, unexpected content, invalid names, and substituted parents are
reported and skipped. Active staging, provider-native caches, configuration,
data, state, and explicit export destinations are outside cleanup authority.
.SH PROVIDER EXECUTION
\&
.PP
Built-in adapters map selected tool installations to native commands. Homebrew
.B formula
and
.B cask
installations use
.BR brew (1);
.B mas
installations use
.BR mas (1).
The
.B uv
adapter supports
.B tool
installations, and the
.B chezmoi
adapter supports
.B target
installations. Their native observation and application commands are
respectively:
.PP
.nf
brew list --formula|--cask --versions LOCATOR
brew install --formula|--cask LOCATOR
mas list
mas install LOCATOR
uv tool list
uv tool install LOCATOR
chezmoi status --path-style=absolute -- LOCATOR
chezmoi apply -- LOCATOR
.fi
.PP
No declaration is needed to enable these built-in identities or their fixed
operations. Optional configured provider arguments precede the native command.
Tool
.B install.arguments
precede the locator. Each configured value remains one literal argument. An
allowed explicit
.B executable
replaces the adapter default.
.PP
A
.B direct-download
.B executable
installation requires an HTTPS locator, absolute destination, and lowercase
.B sha256:
checksum. Observation reads only the destination. Application uses
.BR curl (1)
to write a sibling temporary file, verifies SHA-256, sets executable mode, and
renames it over a regular destination. Rig refuses symlink and non-regular
destinations, restricts redirects to HTTPS, rechecks destination safety before
replacement, and removes failed temporary files.
.SS BUILT-IN MACOS RESOURCES
\&
.PP
On macOS, the built-in
.B launchd
integration owns service and scheduled-job reconciliation without a provider
or action declaration. It renders one LaunchAgent property list beneath
.IR ~/Library/LaunchAgents ,
compares both its content and launchctl load state, replaces safe regular files,
loads or unloads the resolved user domain as declared, and removes stale
managed property lists during retirement. Other platforms report these
resources unavailable without mutation.
.PP
Launchd exposes fixed operations through
.BR "rig run launchd" :
.B status
for a selected service or scheduled job,
.B logs
for either resource with optional
.BR \-\-follow ,
.B restart
for either resource, and
.B run
for a selected scheduled job. Pass the qualified resource after the command's
.B \-\-
separator, for example:
.PP
.nf
rig run launchd logs -- service:example-daemon
rig run launchd run -- scheduled-job:good-morning
.fi
.PP
The built-in
.B macos-defaults
integration observes and applies typed settings. The built-in
.B macos-dock
integration observes and applies a declared semantic Dock order. Native macOS
application inventory is read-only. These integrations require neither
provider tables nor external executables and remain unavailable without
mutation on other platforms.
.SS EXTENSION PROVIDER PROTOCOL
\&
.PP
This protocol is an advanced extension-author interface, not part of ordinary
Rig configuration. An external provider declaration resolves one exact
executable, either from its
.B executable
field or the conventional Rig data path, and requires an exact
.B capabilities
array. Capability values are atomic and are not split on commas.
.PP
Rig invokes an external provider using this versioned literal-argument
protocol:
.PP
.nf
EXECUTABLE [PROVIDER_ARGUMENT ...] rig-provider-v1 VERB PROVIDER TOOL KIND LOCATOR [INSTALL_ARGUMENT ...]
.fi
.PP
.I VERB
is exactly
.B observe
or
.BR apply .
Rig inserts
.B rig-provider-v1
itself; a user never supplies that marker in configuration or a Rig command.
Built-in integrations use internal calls and never receive it.
Rig preserves every configured value as one argument and performs no
evaluation, shell expansion, or implicit word splitting.
.PP
External operational-resource providers use separate exact capabilities
.BR resource-observe ,
.BR resource-apply ,
and
.BR resource-retire .
Selected declarations use this protocol:
.PP
.nf
EXECUTABLE [PROVIDER_ARGUMENT ...] rig-provider-v1 observe-resource PROVIDER ID KIND LOCATOR [FIELD=VALUE ...]
EXECUTABLE [PROVIDER_ARGUMENT ...] rig-provider-v1 apply-resource PROVIDER ID KIND LOCATOR [FIELD=VALUE ...]
EXECUTABLE [PROVIDER_ARGUMENT ...] rig-provider-v1 retire-resource PROVIDER ID KIND LOCATOR previous-managed=true
.fi
.PP
Each scalar is one ordered
.I key=value
argument and each array value repeats its key in declaration order. Providers
receive complete resolved fields and do not parse Rig TOML. Resource-aware
actions append
.B "resource-v1 KIND ID LOCATOR [FIELD=VALUE ...] --"
before remaining caller arguments.
.PP
For a declared external-provider action, Rig fixes
.I KIND
to
.B action
and
.I LOCATOR
to the declared action identity:
.PP
.nf
EXECUTABLE [PROVIDER_ARGUMENT ...] rig-provider-v1 VERB PROVIDER PROVIDER action ACTION
    [ACTION_ARGUMENT ...] [CALLER_ARGUMENT ...]
.fi
.PP
The action declaration authorises that exact provider/action pair. Each caller
argument must
exactly match one
.B allowed-arguments
array item before invocation unless
.B argument-policy
is
.BR provider .
In that case the provider validates its native domain. Provider stdout and
stderr pass through and the native exit status is preserved.
.PP
Publication uses the separate fixed protocol:
.PP
.nf
EXECUTABLE [PROVIDER_ARGUMENT ...] rig-provider-v1 publish PROVIDER PUBLICATION
    directory ABS_EXPORT_DIR
.fi
.PP
An external publisher must declare the exact
.B publish
capability. Rig passes publisher output through and preserves its native result.
.PP
Reverse reconciliation uses a separate fixed protocol that names no tool,
because it enumerates a provider's whole domain rather than observing one
declaration:
.PP
.nf
EXECUTABLE [PROVIDER_ARGUMENT ...] rig-provider-v1 inventory PROVIDER
.fi
.PP
An external provider must declare the exact
.B inventory
capability. Inventory stdout contains zero or more lines, each beginning with
an identity comparable to a tool installation
.BR locator ,
optionally followed by a tab and provider detail that Rig treats as opaque.
The separator is a tab because an identity may be a path containing spaces.
Rig reports every identity that no tool installation declares. Installation
metadata declares an identity either through its
.B locator
, matched only within that installation's own provider namespace because a
Mac App
Store numeric identity and a cask name may coincide, or through a
repeatable tool
.B artifact
value naming a machine-observable path the tool materialises, matched
whichever provider observed it. A
non-zero exit yields state
.B unknown
with detail
.BR exit:N .
Inventory is an observation and must not mutate the machine.
.PP
Observation stdout must contain exactly one of
.BR present ,
.BR missing ,
.BR drifted ,
.BR unavailable ,
or
.BR unknown .
Provider stderr remains diagnostic output. Invalid observation output becomes
.B unknown
with detail
.BR invalid-response ;
a non-zero provider exit becomes
.B unknown
with detail
.BR exit:N .
Application output is routed to stderr so Rig alone owns deterministic stdout.
.SH DEVELOPMENT INSTALLATIONS
\&
.PP
For development against a local checkout, run
.B ./install.sh --link
to link the current executable and manual into
.B RIG_INSTALL_DIR
(default
.IR ~/.local/bin )
and
.B RIG_MAN_INSTALL_DIR
(default: the XDG data directory beneath
.IR man/man1 ).
The linked command follows changes to the checkout and does not download a
release. Re-run link mode after moving the checkout. Use
.B ./bin/rig
directly or
.B man -l man/rig.1
to inspect the checked-out artifacts without changing installed paths.
.SH EXIT STATUS
\&
.B rig run
returns the selected action provider's native status, including values
outside the Rig-owned statuses below.
.B rig publish
likewise returns the publisher's native non-zero status. Interrupted publication
returns 129, 130, or 143 for HUP, INT, or TERM. Rig-owned publication
validation, export, and cleanup failures return 2.
.B rig clean
returns 1 after completing independent safe work when an artifact is skipped
or cannot be removed; invalid syntax or an unsafe cache boundary returns 2.
.TP
.B 0
Healthy status or doctor result, or successful apply or bootstrap, including
neutral catalogue-only rows.
.TP
.B 1
Valid unhealthy observation or doctor finding, provider runtime failure,
dependency suppression, or diagnostic finding. For
.B rig diag
this means configuration is missing or invalid.
.TP
.B 2
Invalid command syntax, unknown query identity, invalid configuration,
resolution failure, or apply or bootstrap preflight failure.
